+We count context frequencies in the unrepeated training corpus and mask the $n$-gram contribution whenever $F\leq\tau$, applying the rule separately to the bigram and trigram branches with thresholds $\tau\in\{1,2,4,8,200\}$. The retrieved embedding is set to zero during both training and evaluation, while the corresponding token remains in the training loss. Compared with unmasked training, suppressing low-frequency memory access reduces the gap (Figure~\ref{fig:low-mask}, left). This experiment supports the relationship between frequency and gap in Section~\ref{sec:forking-factors} and provides a simple way to mitigate forking in the tested setting.
 
 \paragraph{Freezing the tables}
 We train both $n$-gram tables normally during the first epoch, then freeze their parameters while continuing to update the backbone. The learned table contents and context-to-row mapping remain fixed throughout subsequent replay. A pronounced training--validation gap still develops after the tables are frozen (Figure~\ref{fig:table-freeze}, right). Thus, once the tables have learned context representations, other parts of the model can fit to the table which will produce forking, and freezing the tables alone does not prevent it.
